---
// The privacy policy. Written from what the software actually does, and checked against the
// code: what a self-hosted instance sends a network (counts, signed receipts, and for customers a
// first-contact email and the passes their assistants carry), only for networks the owner turns
// on; what our network keeps and for how long; what a customer can do about it; what the hosted
// service would hold; and what the site itself collects.
// It is a draft for a lawyer to review before the hosted service opens, and it says so.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const UPDATED = "23 September 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Surfing Dog"
  description="What Surfing Dog knows about you, which is almost nothing if you run the inbox yourself, and what will change when we host it for you."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="privacy-h">What we know about you</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Surfing Dog Inbox runs on your own server or cloud account, so we know almost nothing about you. This page
      lists exactly what we do see.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: privacy@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="self-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="self-h">If you run the inbox yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Your customers' messages, bookings and orders stay in your database. We never receive them, so we can't read
        or recover them. The code is open if you want to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some things leave your inbox, and only for a <b>network</b> you switch on in Settings → Networks. None is on
        until you switch one on, and you can switch each off again. A network you switch on gets:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Your inbox's address</b>, once, so it can check your inbox's public profile, and <b>every hour</b> your
          software version, what it runs on, and how many bookings, orders, quotes and messages arrived in the last 24
          hours. Counts only. With your secret key set, this hourly message is signed, and the network answers it with
          your own standing there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Every receipt your inbox signs</b>: when a booking is confirmed or an order accepted or paid, and again
          when it ends (it happened, the customer did not turn up, either side cancelled, it was delivered). A receipt
          says which business issued it, your reference for the booking or order, what kind of promise it is, when it
          was due and when it was made, the amount if there is a price, and how an order was paid when your inbox
          knows. The customer appears as a code, never a name: an HMAC made with a key only your inbox holds, which
          the network can't turn back into a person, and the same customer at another business gets a different
          code. When the customer's assistant presented their pass, the receipt also names that presentation, one
          per network, so the network that issued the pass counts the outcome on that person's record; every network
          the receipt goes to sees these references.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A counter-signature</b>, if the customer's assistant signs a receipt: its signature and its public key.
          An assistant that keeps its key uses the same one at every business, so the receipts it signed can be
          linked to each other. It may also name the customer's pass, by a reference that is not the pass itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A new customer's email address</b>, when a customer books or orders with an email and their assistant
          carries no pass. With it go your reference for that booking or order and the name of their assistant
          (and a fingerprint of its public key, and its platform, when it signs). The network uses the address to give the customer a
          <b>key</b>, a secret that lets any business on that network recognise them later; your inbox emails the key
          to the customer as one line in your name, a code for their assistant, and names no network. Turn the line
          off under Customers you already know; turn the key off for a network with "Give first-time customers a key".
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A pass or a key the customer's assistant carries</b>, only to the network that issued it, with the
          customer's email address when their request has one, so the network can say how that customer keeps their
          bookings and whether the address is theirs. An assistant that signs its requests carries a reference
          instead of the pass, and your inbox passes on its signature: the address, path and query of the request, a
          hash of what it sent, its content type, its public key and the pass references it signed. Your inbox never
          passes on a signature that holds a secret, or that covers any other header.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Never sent: message text, names, phone numbers, postal addresses, what was ordered, payment references.
      </p>
      <p>
        What your inbox keeps of this, in your database: what each network said about a customer (a code for that
        customer at your business, and their standing) on the booking or order and on the customer; a hash of each
        pass it saw, to recognise it again; a network's answers for an hour, a day for "already known", filed under
        hashes. A key a customer's assistant presents is passed on and never stored. A new customer's key is kept
        sealed with your secret key until it is emailed to them, and their first pass sealed so the assistant that
        made the booking or order can collect it again; neither longer than seven days, and neither shown to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn a network off and nothing more is sent to it. What it already holds, it keeps under its own rules. Anyone
        can run a network, and one we don't run is not covered by this page: add only networks you trust.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">If we host it for you</h2>
      <p>
        Hosting is not open yet. When it opens, you will be the controller of the data your customers send you, and we
        will be your processor. We will hold it on our own servers in the European Union and use it only to run the
        service for you. For your own account, your address, your billing and how you use the service, we will be the
        controller.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will be able to export everything as a file, and delete everything, from the software without asking us.
        A written processing agreement will be part of the terms before the first customer is onboarded.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">This website</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, advertising, tracking pixels or third-party scripts, and no cookie banner, because there is
        nothing to consent to. Our servers keep ordinary web logs, including your IP address and the page you asked
        for, for a short time to keep the site up and stop abuse. Cloudflare sits in front of the site and keeps its own
        logs for the same reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you join the waitlist or write to us, we keep your message and email address so we can answer, and use them
        for nothing else. Posts on the requests board are public. The email address you give there is used only to
        tell you when your request ships.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to correct or delete it, object to what
        we do with it, and complain to a supervisory authority. For the network, deleting means we remove what links a
        record to you (your email's code, your keys and passes); the coded records of past bookings stay, because
        businesses' records are made of them. In Portugal that is the
        <a class="link" href="https://www.cnpd.pt/" rel="noopener">CNPD</a>. Write to privacy@surfingdog.ai and we will
        answer within a month.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where we rely on legitimate interest rather than your consent, it is to keep the service running and prevent
        abuse, and you can object to that too.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section" id="network-h">Our network, and the customers of businesses on it</h2>
      <p>
        We run the network at <code>network.surfingdog.ai</code>. Businesses whose inbox reports to it are listed in
        its directory, and it keeps a record of how reliably businesses and their customers keep bookings and orders.
        It uses only real transactions, and it never refuses or blocks anyone: it orders its directory by rules it
        publishes, and each business's own rules decide what a customer's record earns them, rules that may only
        speed things up or bring in a person, never refuse.
      </p>
      <p><b>What it keeps, and for how long.</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The hourly messages, with the internet address they came from, for 90 days. A business's listing (its public
          profile, version, whether it is answering, its counts and its standing), and snapshots of it, for good.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every receipt, counter-signature, and record of which business was shown which customer's standing, for
          good. A record that could quietly disappear would be worth nothing, so none does.
        </li>
        <li>
          For a customer: a record with no name, keyed by an HMAC of their email address, and an HMAC of its domain
          (never the address itself); the hashes of their key and passes (never the secrets, except a new customer's
          key and pass, kept encrypted for seven days so a retry never loses them, and a pass made from a key, kept
          encrypted for a day); the name, public key and platform of the assistants that carried
          them; and the outcomes of their bookings and orders, from receipts that name them by a code.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sign-in sessions for 24 hours, and emailed codes for ten minutes. A code is sent to the address as typed,
          and the address is not stored.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>Who sees what.</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A business's standing (its score and tier, and how many promises it kept and broke) is public, in the
          directory. Nothing about any customer is ever in it.
        </li>
        <li>
          A customer's standing is seen by a business only when the customer's assistant presents their pass there.
          There is no way to look a person up. The business sees their tier, their score, how many promises they kept
          and broke and at how many businesses, since when the network has known them, whether they proved their
          email address, and whether their pass was used unusually. It never sees their email address or which other
          businesses they used, and each business gets a different code for the same person.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>If you are a customer.</b></p>
      <p>
        You never sign up. If you book or order with an email at a business on the network, you may get an email with
        your key. You can give it to any assistant, or ignore it. With a code sent to your address you can sign in at
        the network to see your record, every business that was shown it, and the passes your assistants hold; revoke
        a pass; replace a lost key (your record carries over); and contest an outcome you think is wrong. Or write to
        us.
      </p>
      <p>
        The network's rules, in plain words and as numbers, are on <a class="link" href="/network/">the network page</a>
        and at <code>network.surfingdog.ai/v1/ranking</code>, with every change announced fifteen days before it
        applies.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        The date at the top shows the last change. The site is an open repository, so every edit to this page is a
        public commit. We will announce material changes on the blog.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">Where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          We wrote this from what the software does today and checked it against the code. A lawyer has not reviewed
          it yet. That happens before hosting opens to anyone but us, and this note comes off the page once it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
